---
import ContentShell from '../../layouts/ContentShell.astro'
import { allJobs, loadAll, stats } from '../../data/load'
import { SITE_NAME } from '../../data/seo'
import { url } from '../../data/slugs'
import { GUIDES, articleLd, guideUrl, otherGuides } from '../../data/guides'

const guide = GUIDES.find((g) => g.slug === 'pune-startup-jobs-for-freshers')!

const jobs = await allJobs()
const companies = await loadAll()
const s = await stats()

const feeds = companies.filter((c) => c.ats)
const atsNames = [...new Set(feeds.map((c) => c.ats))].sort()
const fresher = jobs.filter((j) => j.experience_max !== null && j.experience_max <= 2)
const interns = jobs.filter((j) => j.employment_type === 'internship' || j.experience_max === 0)
const freshCompanies = [...new Set(fresher.map((j) => j.company.name))]

const description = guide.blurb
---

<ContentShell
  title={`${guide.title} | ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={description}
  path={guideUrl(guide.slug)}
  h1={guide.h1}
  lede={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: guide.nav, href: guideUrl(guide.slug) }]}
  jsonLd={[articleLd(guide, Astro.site!, SITE_NAME)]}
  narrow
>
  <p>
    Pune has two ways in for a fresher, and they work nothing alike. The services and GCC
    campuses in Hinjawadi and Kharadi hire graduates in the hundreds through aptitude tests and
    campus drives, months ahead of a start date. The product startups this site tracks do the
    opposite: they hire a handful of juniors at a time, when a team has the room to bring one up,
    and they decide on what you can actually build rather than on a percentile. This guide is
    about the second route.
  </p>

  <h2>What is open at this level right now</h2>
  <p>
    {fresher.length > 0 ? (
      <>
        {fresher.length} of the {s.jobs} roles open across the tracked startups ask for two years
        of experience or less, and {interns.length} {interns.length === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} tagged
        as an internship. They are collected on{' '}
        <a href="/fresher-jobs-in-pune/">fresher jobs in Pune</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/internships-in-pune/">internships in Pune</a>, rebuilt from the feeds every six
        hours.
        {freshCompanies.length > 0 && (
          <> Hiring at this level today: {freshCompanies.slice(0, 10).join(', ')}
            {freshCompanies.length > 10 ? ' and others' : ''}.</>
        )}
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        Nothing at two years or less is open across the tracked startups this morning. That is
        normal rather than a gap — startup fresher hiring runs in bursts, and the{' '}
        <a href="/fresher-jobs-in-pune/">fresher jobs page</a> refills when a crawl finds new
        roles. The reliable way to catch them is the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>, which lists
        a role the same day it is published.
      </>
    )}
  </p>

  <h2>Why the list is short</h2>
  <p>
    A startup opens a junior role when it has someone with the time to mentor and work steady
    enough to hand over. That is a narrower condition than a large company faces, so the openings
    come in ones and twos and dry up between them. It is not a measure of how much a company is
    hiring overall — a team can be growing fast and still have no fresher seat open, because every
    senior is busy shipping. Read a short list as "not this week", not "not here".
  </p>

  <h2>What entry-level startup roles actually ask for</h2>
  <p>
    The titles vary — SDE-1, Associate Engineer, Graduate Engineer Trainee, Junior Developer,
    Intern — and mean roughly the same thing: someone in their first year or two who can already
    write working code. What moves an application at this level is evidence you can build. One
    finished project with a link — a deployed app, a merged pull request to something real, a
    repository that is more than a tutorial — outweighs a strong CGPA on its own, because it
    answers the only question the team has, which is whether you can do the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Experience levels on this site are inferred from the role title and description by pattern
    matching, not read from a structured field the employer filled in, so the filter is a way to
    narrow a list rather than a rule about who may apply. Startups here interview people a year
    short of what a posting asks for more often than a portal would suggest. If you meet most of
    the must-haves, apply. How that inference works, and where it is wrong, is in{' '}
    <a href={url.about()}>how this works</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Applying through a company ATS is not applying on Naukri</h2>
  <p>
    Every apply button here goes to the employer's own hiring system —{' '}
    {atsNames.length ? atsNames.join(', ') : 'the company ATS'} — not to an inbox this site owns
    and not to a recruiter who decides whether to pass you on. There is no intermediary and no
    database selling your profile on. The trade-off is that spraying does not work: a generic
    application to a five-person team is obvious and ignored. The move that works is the opposite
    of the portal habit — read the posting, say in two lines why this specific role, and link to
    something you made. Ten applications done that way beat a hundred one-click ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Being early beats being thorough</h2>
  <p>
    Junior roles at small teams close fast, sometimes within days, because a good early
    application ends the search. The practical edge is speed. New roles land on{' '}
    <a href={url.jobs()}>the latest roles page</a> and in the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a> as
    soon as a crawl picks them up, so a feed reader will tell you about an opening the morning it
    appears. Checking a portal once a week is how you find out about it after it closed.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    There is no salary figure here. The feeds this site reads almost never carry a pay band, so
    any number on this page would be invented, and inventing one is worse than the gap. Ask about
    compensation directly once you are talking to the team — a startup that will not discuss it
    early is telling you something.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top:2em">Other guides</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {otherGuides(guide.slug).map((g) => (
      <a class="tile" href={guideUrl(g.slug)}>
        <h3>{g.nav}</h3>
        <span class="meta">{g.blurb}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
</ContentShell>
